1) Recommended — Mock the `UserService` and use `@WithMockUser`
Why: fast, stable, keeps the test focused on controller behavior (mapping and injection) without touching DB or token logic.

Example (add to your test class):
```java
import static org.mockito.Mockito.when;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.get;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.status;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.jsonPath;

import org.springframework.boot.test.mock.mockito.MockBean;
import org.springframework.security.test.context.support.WithMockUser;

// in class body
@MockBean
private UserService userService;

@Test
@WithMockUser(username = "testuser", roles = "USER")
public void testGetCurrentUser_withMockedService() throws Exception {
    UserDto userDto = new UserDto();
    userDto.setUsername("testuser");
    userDto.setEmail("test@example.com");
    // set other fields as needed

    when(userService.getUserByUsername("testuser")).thenReturn(userDto);

    mvc.perform(get("/api/users/me").accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
       .andDo(print())
       .andExpect(status().isOk())
       .andExpect(jsonPath("$.username").value("testuser"))
       .andExpect(jsonPath("$.email").value("test@example.com"));
}
```
Notes:
- `@WithMockUser` ensures `@AuthenticationPrincipal UserDetails userDetails` is populated with a principal having username "testuser".
- `@MockBean` injects a mock `UserService` into the application context so the controller calls the stubbed method.

